all this commands are for terminal
CLONE->
it is a command used in terminal as git clone ____link____ for copying that entire repo from 
our github to the particular folder
cd->
used to change directory 
clear->
used to clear the terminal
ls->
means list files ,used to view all files in that folder
ls -a ->
shows all files including hidden files
if it is a git file this will show git else not
*ls -a (for gitbash)
*ls -Force(for powershell)

status ->
using git status in terminal shows whether the folder is in its original state as in github
types of status->
untracked:-new files that git doesnt track
modified:-changed
staged:-file ready to be committed (file added)
unmodified:-unchanged(file committed)

here when we added index.html it was untracked 
add->
add new or changed file in the git staging area
so,when we did git add index.html in terminal file becomes staged
git add . adds all files to git staging area

commit->
it is the record of change
git commit -m"some message"

push->
now any changes done locally(local repo) in our system wont be visible in github(remote repo)
therefore we use push command,
which upload local repo to remote repo
git push origin main (here origin is the name of the remote repo through which we copied our code)

* cd .. is used to get out of that directory
* mkdir <-directory name-> used to make directory
init->
used to create a new git repo or to add the repo in git 
before doing init to any file go inside and check with ls -a/ls -Force if it has git
if not than use init
git init 

*create new repo in github

git remote add origin <-link->
adds that repo as the origin or simply connects the new repo made in github with our local repo

git remote -v
to verify remote

git branch 
to check branch

git branch -M main (to rename branch as main)

git push origin main (to push code in github) or
git push -u origin main (so every change in future is always pushed in origin main)


GIT WORKFLOW for local git
github repo -> clone -> changes -> add -> commit -> push


GIT BRANCHES->
BRANCH COMMANDS:-
git branch (gives branch we are working on)
git branch -M main (renames branch)
git checkout -b <-new branch name-> (to create new branch)
git checkout <-branch name-> (navigate to another branch)
git branch -d <-branch name-> (to delete branch)

whenever we move to any branch other than main and do any changes there,add and commit them
and then move to main branch then changes only reflect in other branch(feature1)not in main branch
and then we will -> git push origin feature1


MERGING CODE
WAY 1
for say we are on  feature 1 branch and we want to merge it with main branch
then first we will see the difference between them through command
git diff main
then to merge them both use command
git merge main

WAY 2
through pull request(pr)
in github we will select feature 1 branch and contribute and merge pull request
this will check both codes and merge them both
but they will be merged only in remote repo not in local repo so we will run in terminal
git pull origin main

RESOLVING MERGE CONFLICTS
for say, the main branch has 
<p>this is a feature(button)</p>
the feature branch has 
<p>this is a feature(dropdown)</p>
merging them will raise conflict which one to chose
so here we will merge through the way 1
using git merge main